Today I went deeper into Solana's account model and started understanding the hierarchy behind different types of accounts.
The interesting part is that a wallet, a program, and a sysvar may look completely different conceptually, but they all follow the same basic account structure:
lamports • data • owner • executable • rent_epoch
What clicked for me was understanding the role of the System Program.
A normal wallet account is owned by the System Program:

Wallet
   ↓
System Program
   ↓
Creates accounts + handles SOL transfers

Then there are native programs, which are executable accounts, and sysvar accounts, which provide read-only information about the current state of the Solana network.
The Web2 analogy helped a lot:
System Program - OS kernel
 Sysvars -  /proc
 Wallet - user-space process
The more I learn about Solana's account hierarchy, the more the pieces from the previous days start connecting.